<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/advanced/psychometrics-and-testing">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced lesson in Psychology, and it draws on the whole ladder beneath it. The
    foundational lesson <em>Memory: How We Remember</em> gave you psychological constructs worth
    measuring; the intermediate lessons <em>Research Methods</em>, <em>Personality Theories</em>
    and <em>Abnormal Psychology &amp; Therapies</em> taught you how evidence is collected, where
    trait scores come from, and what diagnosis demands. This lesson supplies the machinery those
    lessons quietly assumed: how a number like an IQ score or a personality scale is built, and
    how much trust it has earned.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define reliability and validity, and explain why the first bounds the second but never guarantees it;</li>
    <li>compute a standard error of measurement and say what a reported score honestly means;</li>
    <li>reason about percentiles, base rates and screening with Bayesian arithmetic;</li>
    <li>state what g is, what it predicts, and which interpretive claims remain genuinely contested;</li>
    <li>interrogate any test — clinical, educational or corporate — with a psychometrician's checklist, including its history of misuse.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Psychometrics does what no other science must: measure properties that cannot be laid on a bench
    — intelligence, personality, depression, attitudes — and prove the measurement trustworthy. Its
    core tools are statistical, its standards are ruthless (a psychological claim is only as good as
    the instrument behind it), and its history contains both psychology's proudest quantitative
    achievements and its most damaging ethical failures. By the end of this chapter you should be
    able to interrogate any test — clinical, educational, or corporate — with the same checklist a
    psychometrician would.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the measurement standards and their hardest case — IQ" widget="equationStep" config={psychCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> press "Walk me through it" and reveal the four steps —
    they run from the abstract standard to the hardest concrete case and then to the
    discipline's own cautionary history. Steps 1 and 2 give you the vocabulary every later
    section uses (reliability, validity, g); steps 3 and 4 show the same instrument producing
    the field's proudest quantitative result and its most damaging ethical failures.
  </p>

  <h2>Reliability: consistency first</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Classical test theory</strong> models every observed score as X = T + E — true score plus
    error. Reliability is the proportion of variance that is true-score variance: r<sub>xx</sub> =
    σ<sub>T</sub>² / σ<sub>X</sub>², estimated three ways: <em>test-retest</em> (same people, same
    test, weeks apart — measures temporal stability), <em>parallel forms</em> (two equivalent
    versions), and <em>internal consistency</em> (do the items hang together? — Cronbach's alpha,
    effectively the average inter-item correlation). Two structural facts follow. First, an
    unreliable measure cannot be valid: validity is bounded by the square root of reliability, so a
    test with reliability 0.64 has a maximum possible validity correlation of 0.80. Second, high
    reliability is cheap and says nothing about validity — ten reworded versions of the same bad
    question will give alpha ≈ 0.95 while measuring a tautology. The standard in applied testing is
    reliability of 0.80 or better; high-stakes decisions want 0.90+.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Worked example.</strong> An IQ test has reliability 0.90 and SD 15. The standard error
    of measurement is SEM = SD × √(1 − r<sub>xx</sub>) = 15 × √0.10 ≈ 4.7 points. So a reported
    IQ of 124 is, at 95% confidence, roughly 124 ± 9 (two SEMs) — a band from about 115 to 133.
    This is why professional practice reports confidence intervals and why a four-point difference
    between two children's scores on a good test is noise: the SEM, not the point estimate, carries
    the decision weight.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a reported IQ of 124 means the person's intelligence
  "is" 124 — an exact quantity like a height in centimetres. The score is an estimate with
  a band: on a good test, 124 is honestly 115-133 at 95% confidence, and the same child
  retested next month may land several points either way. Treating point scores as exact
  attributes is how four-point differences between applicants get mistaken for real
  differences in ability.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> every score is an estimate with an error band, and consistency is
    the price of admission — a test that cannot agree with itself cannot measure anything. The
    next section asks the harder question: agreeing about <em>what</em>? That is validity, and
    it comes in faces rather than one.
  </p>

  <h2>Validity: the multi-faced standard</h2>
  <p>
    Validity is not a property a test <em>has</em>; it is the strength of evidence that scores
    support a particular interpretation. The modern framework (Messick, APA standards) recognises
    facets rather than types: <strong>content</strong> (do items sample the domain? a maths exam that
    is all arithmetic lacks content validity for maths), <strong>criterion</strong> (do scores predict
    a real outcome? — concurrent or predictive), and <strong>construct</strong> (does the test behave
    as the theory says? convergent correlations with related measures, discriminant non-correlations
    with unrelated ones — the multitrait-multimethod matrix is the classic design). Two traps
    deserve names. <strong>Criterion contamination</strong>: validating a job test against supervisor
    ratings when supervisors know the test scores — the criterion is already infected.
    <strong>Construct proliferation</strong>: rebranding reliable instruments as new constructs;
    the history of "emotional intelligence" measures is the cautionary tale — reliability was never
    the problem, incremental validity over existing traits was.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a reliable test is a good test." Reliability is the floor, not
    the building. The most consequential failures in testing history — and in everyday self-report
    questionnaires — are reliably valid-looking instruments with no validated interpretation attached.
  </p>

  <h2>Scaling, norms, and the mathematics of rank</h2>
  <p>
    Raw scores mean nothing without a normative frame. Standardisation converts raw scores to
    positions in a reference distribution: z = (x − mean)/SD, and IQ deliberately anchors at mean
    100, SD 15 (deviation IQ, replacing the ratio IQ of mental-age ÷ chronological-age, which
    broke down in adults). The percentiles follow from the normal curve: 115 is ~84th percentile
    (one SD), 130 is ~98th (two SD), 145 is 99.9th (three SD — about 3 people in 1,000, which is
    why gifted-programme cutoffs are administratively, not psychologically, meaningful boundaries).
    The tail behaviour matters for practice: at the extremes the normal curve is a poor model of
    true ability and small measurement errors move percentiles violently, one reason high-stakes
    individual decisions require multiple indicators.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Base rates complete the toolkit.</strong> Any diagnostic cut faces the Bayesian trap.
    A screening instrument with 95% sensitivity and 95% specificity applied to a condition with
    prevalence 1 in 1,000 yields: from 1,000 people, 1 true positive and about 50 false positives —
    a positive result means roughly a 1-in-51 chance (2%) of having the condition. The arithmetic
    does not care how good the test sounds; prevalence sets the ceiling. Every screening debate —
    medical, psychiatric, security — is this calculation wearing different clothes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a raw score means nothing until it is placed in a distribution,
    and a cutoff means nothing until prevalence has had its say. With those tools in hand the
    next section takes on the field's most robust — and most contested — construct: the general
    factor g.
  </p>

  <h2>g: factor analysis and its discontents</h2>
  <p>
    Spearman noticed in 1904 that all cognitive tests correlate positively with each other — the
    <strong>positive manifold</strong> — and factor analysis extracts from that correlation matrix
    a general factor, <strong>g</strong>, on which every subtest loads. The empirical claims are
    among psychology's most robust: g predicts scholastic achievement (r ≈ 0.5–0.7 with standardised
    outcomes) and job performance (meta-analytic r ≈ 0.5, rising with job complexity), and g-loading
    rises with subtests' abstractness. The interpretive claims remain contested — precisely the
    field's live frontier. Candidate accounts: g as a single biological efficiency (processing speed,
    working-memory capacity — current best correlate is executive/WM function, not raw speed); g as
    statistical residue of many overlapping processes (van der Maas's <em>mutualism</em> model: gains
    in one ability feed the others, generating the manifold without a common cause); g as sampling
    of learned skills. Heritability is substantial and rises with age (twin-study estimates around
    0.5 in childhood, 0.7–0.8 in adulthood in WEIRD samples) — while the Flynn effect demonstrates
    massive environmental malleability across cohorts, and adoption studies show family-environment
    effects. Both are true; heritability within a population constrains nothing about change between
    environments, the standard heritability misreading this course exists to kill.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "heritability of 0.8" means 80% of a person's
  intelligence came from their genes and 20% from their environment — a partition that
  could be computed per individual. Heritability is a population statistic: the share of
  variation <em>between people in a particular environment</em> attributable to genetic
  differences. Make the environment more equal and heritability rises; make it wildly
  unequal and it falls — which is how a trait can be strongly heritable and still shift
  thirty points in three generations, as the Flynn effect showed.</p>

  <h2>Fairness, misuse, and the honest history</h2>
  <p>
    The misuse is documented, not hypothetical: Yerkes's Army Alpha/Beta tests fed the 1924
    immigration-restriction debate with mis-scored and mis-translated results; eugenics movements
    across a dozen countries cited intelligence testing. The modern discipline's response is
    structural rather than rhetorical: <strong>item bias and DIF analysis</strong> (differential item
    functioning: does the item behave differently for matched-ability groups? — flags biased items
    statistically), <strong>predictive-bias studies</strong> (the technical finding, replicated
    across decades: cognitive tests predict outcomes with approximately equal slope for major
    demographic groups in employment settings, though intercept debates persist — which does not
    settle the deeper question of what the test scores' social meaning should be),
    <strong>stereotype threat</strong> as situational performance variance (replication-size debates
    ongoing), and <strong>professional standards</strong> (qualification requirements, adverse-impact
    law in employment testing). The mature position: psychometrics is a measurement technology whose
    outputs are only as ethical as the decisions they feed — the instrument, the interpretation and
    the institution each need separate scrutiny.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> misuse is part of this discipline's record, and the modern
    response is structural — bias analysis, professional standards, separate scrutiny of
    instrument, interpretation and institution. The next section shows that machinery operating
    end to end, from the first item written to the score report a family receives.
  </p>

  <h2>The life of a test: from blueprint to score report</h2>
  <p>
    A modern standardised test is engineered end-to-end. A <strong>test blueprint</strong>
    specifies content domains and cognitive levels, with items sampled to match — content
    validity by design, not afterthought. Items are written to specification, reviewed for
    bias, and <strong>pretested</strong> on representative samples: difficulty
    (p-values), discrimination (item-total correlations), distractor functioning and DIF
    screening all happen before any item counts toward a score — items that misbehave are
    discarded invisibly. <strong>Scaling and equating</strong> place raw scores on the
    reported scale: IRT anchors new forms to old ones so scores mean the same thing across
    years. <strong>Reliability estimation</strong> uses the operational data — alpha, or IRT
    information functions, which quantify precision <em>at each ability level</em>, the
    refinement classical reliability misses. <strong>Cut scores</strong> — pass marks — are
    set by expert panels using documented methods (Angoff, Bookmark), and the judgement
    content of that step is the dirty secret of every certification exam: the statistics
    standardise, the standard is human. The score report then carries standard-error bands
    and interpretation guidance, because the professionals' first duty is communicating
    precision honestly.
  </p>

  <h2>Self-report and its discontents</h2>
  <p>
    Personality and attitude measurement runs on self-report, and its failure modes are
    well-mapped: <strong>response styles</strong> (acquiescence, social desirability — with
    Marlowe-Crowne-type scales measuring the latter and forced-choice formats controlling it),
    <strong>faking</strong> (applicant personality testing: coaching works, so validity in
    hiring samples runs below honest-sample validity), and the <strong>reference-group
    effect</strong> (ratings are relative to local norms — the "modesty artefacts" of
    cross-cultural comparison). The construct-level findings survive: the Big Five's
    cross-cultural structure, its predictive validity for job performance (conscientiousness
    at meta-analytic r ≈ 0.2–0.3) and health outcomes, moderate heritability, and mean-level
    change with age (the maturity principle). The modern settlement: self-report instruments
    are valid enough to be useful and unreliable enough to need corroboration — informant
    reports, behavioural tasks, situational-judgment tests and objective criteria triangulate,
    which is exactly what serious selection and clinical assessment do.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why does low reliability make validity impossible, while high reliability guarantees nothing?</li>
    <li>Two applicants' scores on a test with reliability 0.90 and SD 15 differ by four points. Using the standard error of measurement, explain what that difference does and does not license you to conclude.</li>
    <li>A screening test is 95% sensitive and 95% specific for a condition affecting 1 person in 1,000. Why does a positive result still leave you more likely than not to be healthy?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Validity is bounded by the square root of reliability, so a
    test that cannot even agree with itself has no consistency for any interpretation to attach
    to; but error-free agreement is equally compatible with measuring a tautology — ten
    reworded versions of one bad question reach alpha near 0.95 while possibly measuring
    nothing of interest. (2) The SEM is 15 × the square root of 0.10, roughly 4.7 points, so a
    four-point gap is inside one standard error — retesting could reverse the order. What it
    does not license is treating the ranks as real ability differences; professional practice
    reports confidence bands precisely so decisions rest on the band, not the point estimate.
    (3) Because prevalence sets the base: out of 1,000 people, about 1 true positive is
    outweighed by the 5% false-positive rate applied to the roughly 999 healthy — about 50
    false positives — so a positive result shifts the odds from 1 in 1,000 to about 1 in 51,
    and only confirmatory testing moves them further.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every observed score is true score plus error; reliability is the share of variance that is signal.</li>
    <li>Reliability bounds validity from below but never certifies it — a reliable test can be reliably measuring the wrong thing.</li>
    <li>Validity is the strength of evidence for an interpretation, judged across content, criterion and construct facets.</li>
    <li>Scores are positions in distributions: SEM bands and percentiles carry the decision weight, not point estimates.</li>
    <li>Base rates set the ceiling on every cutoff — Bayesian arithmetic governs all screening debates.</li>
    <li>g is psychology's most robust predictive finding, and its interpretation is the field's live frontier, not a settled fact.</li>
    <li>Heritability is a population statistic within an environment; the Flynn effect shows what environments can move.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling advanced lessons pick up both halves of this one:
    <em>Cognitive Psychology &amp; Models</em> examines the constructs — working memory,
    executive function — that cognitive tests increasingly measure, while
    <em>Neuropsychology</em> shows what happens to measurement when the substrate itself is
    damaged, where a lesion is both the diagnosis and the experiment.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Item response theory replaced the classical toolkit.</strong> Where classical theory
    treats every item equally, IRT models each item's own response function: probability of correct
    response as a logistic curve of ability with parameters for difficulty and discrimination (and
    guessing). The consequences are everywhere in modern testing: <strong>computerised adaptive
    testing</strong> (the GRE, adaptive sections) selects the next item at your current ability
    estimate, halving test length at equal precision; test equating across forms and years becomes
    principled; and item banks can be audited for DIF at scale. <strong>Modern validity theory</strong>
    has moved to argument-based validation (Kane): specify the claims, test each link, treat validity
    as an evaluation of the argument. <strong>The Flynn effect's reversal</strong> (Norway, Finland,
    the UK show declines of a few points per decade since the 1990s among the native-born) is
    genuinely unexplained — candidate mechanisms run from education and media environments to
    within-family secular trends; Bratsberg and Rogeberg's within-family analysis showed the
    reversal is environmental, not compositional, and stopped there. <strong>Open questions:</strong>
    what g actually is (process-overlap and network models versus unitary-cause models is a real
    mathematical dispute, not a verbal one); whether adaptive digital assessment can measure
    competencies (collaboration, creativity) that traditional item formats miss; how large language
    models change both test administration and test security; and whether psychological measurement
    can ever escape its WEIRD norming base — most published norms still describe the populations
    least representative of humanity.
  </p>
  <p>
    The ethics of measurement decisions deserve their own paragraph, because the statistics
    are never the whole decision. Every cut score, every risk band, every actuarial tool
    embeds a value judgement about false positives versus false negatives — who gets screened
    further, who gets hired, who gets flagged — and the moral arithmetic (how many false
    alarms justify how many catches) is society's, not the psychometrician's. Professional
    practice handles this by making the trade-offs explicit: report the confusion matrix at
    the chosen threshold, publish the adverse-impact analysis, state what the instrument does
    not measure. The historical failures recited earlier — eugenics, biased immigration
    scoring — were failures of exactly this honesty: statistics presented as if they settled
    normative questions. The mature field's contribution is keeping the two separable:
    rigorous measurement informing, but never replacing, the accountable human decision — a
    boundary every algorithmic assessment system now inherits as its first design constraint.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself: alpha, percentiles, base rates, Flynn logic" widget="quiz" config={psyQuiz} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> commit to an answer before opening each explanation — the
    distractors are exactly the confident mistakes the lesson dissected, from treating
    reliability as validity to ignoring base rates. On a miss, redo the arithmetic rather than
